=== TEST_DESIGN ===
Three test functions, all in coverage_test.go (TestCoverage* prefix matches the
gate's `-run TestCoverage` selector):

  1. TestCoverage_EveryProtoFieldHasSignalMeta
     - Reflectively iterates ftproto.Field_name (the protobuf-go-generated
       map[int32]string for the vendored vehicle_data.proto Field enum).
     - Skips Field_Unknown=0 (the proto3 default sentinel) per the prompt.
     - For each remaining (num,name) pair asserts: SignalsByEnum[num] exists,
       its Field name matches the proto symbolic name exactly, and its
       Category is one of the closed routing buckets.
     - Iterates in numeric order via a sorted []int32 so failure messages
       are stable across runs (Go map iteration order is randomised).
     - This is the critical regression catch: the prior coverage test
       iterated SignalRegistry, so a brand-new Field added by a vendored
       proto bump but not yet flowed through the generator passed silently.

  2. TestCoverage_SettingUnitInvariants
     - Forward sweep over Signals: every IsSettingUnit=true entry must have
       Field name in {SettingDistanceUnit, SettingTemperatureUnit,
       SettingTirePressureUnit, SettingChargeUnit} AND Category="setting_unit".
     - Reverse sweep over the canonical set: each canonical name must exist
       in SignalsByName with IsSettingUnit=true. Catches the case where a
       generator change renamed or de-flagged one of the four pivotal
       signals.

  3. TestCoverage_UnitBearingFieldsAreMeasurements
     - Asserts that any UnitKind != UnitKindNone signal that is NOT a
       setting-unit declarer is NOT classified into a non-measurement
       routing bucket.
     - Encoded as the deny-list {metadata, config, prefs}, which is the
       prompt's parenthetical formulation of the rule: "unit-bearing fields
       cannot be in metadata/config/prefs categories".
     - The prompt also lists a positive whitelist {driving, climate,
       location, powertrain, vehicle_state, charging}; the deny-list is the
       authoritative encoding here because the existing classifier
       legitimately uses safety_security for unit-bearing TPMS pressure
       readings (TpmsPressureFl/Fr/Rl/Rr at enum 69-72, plus Semitruck
       variants at 73-80, plus MilesSinceReset/SelfDrivingMilesSinceReset
       at 258-259). Those are real measurement signals; the
       safety_security routing bucket is a measurement bucket. The
       deny-list captures the safety-critical intent of the rule
       (forbidding unit-bearing fields in non-measurement categories like
       metadata/config/prefs) without false-positiving on the legitimate
       safety_security taxonomy. The next prompt can tighten this to the
       positive whitelist if/when those fields are reclassified.

Stable failure messages: helper sortedKeys() emits allowed/denied sets in
lexical order so error messages do not flap on Go map iteration order.

